@flexiarg creativity-commons/formative-sanctions
@title Formative Sanctions
@keywords sanctions, critique, feedback, review, audience response, Ostrom 5
@audience reviewers, maintainers, operators
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 5 (graduated sanctions), as transposed in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016), Table 2 and section "5. Graduated sanctions" (the IDEA cycle, Colton, Charnley and Pease 2011). Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29. Derived from the paper, not mined from runs.
@transposes institutions/graduated-sanctions
@status draft 2026-10-29; runs correct it
@references [creativity-commons/ARGUMENT institutions/rules-as-institutional-statements creativity-commons/low-cost-local-conflict-arenas]

! conclusion:
  Producers who violate operational rules are assessed sanctions by
  other Producers, graduated by seriousness and context, and mostly
  formative rather than punitive.

  + context:
    Economic sanctions are punishments with a presumed direct effect. In
    creative and productive settings a "sanction" ranges from a
    constructive review to outright condemnation to no response at all,
    and the response shapes the Producer. Feedback from the Producer's
    "parent" matters most.

  + IF:
    A Producer's contribution breaks a rule or falls short.

  + HOWEVER:
    No response is itself a sanction and the hardest to read. Heavy
    sanctions for small faults drive contributors away; none at all lets
    the rule lapse.

  + THEN:
    Grade the response: a note, then a fix made by the reviewer with the
    reason recorded, then a changed rule or test, then restricted access.
    Always give some response that the Producer can read, and say which
    step it is.

  + BECAUSE:
    Graduated, legible responses teach; they let a Producer correct
    course before the cost is large, and they keep the rule alive.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Example: reviews in the fix hour fixed five accepted patches directly (16533, 7413, 27239, 10618, 20650), each with an added test that fails on the pre-review version.]
